+
+# SynFlowNet
+
+![SynFlowNet](https://proto-bio.github.io/proto-assets/images/tool/synflownet/hero.png)
+
+> [!NOTE]
+> **License:** SynFlowNet is open source and free for academic and commercial use under an MIT license. Please refer to [the license](https://github.com/mirunacrt/synflownet/blob/main/LICENSE) for full terms. Trained checkpoints are not distributed; users provision their own weights by training with the upstream repository.
+
+## Overview
+
+[SynFlowNet](https://github.com/mirunacrt/synflownet) is a generative flow network (GFlowNet) that designs small molecules over a synthesis action space, assembling each molecule from purchasable building blocks and chemical reactions so that every sample arrives with an explicit synthesis route. This toolkit loads a trained SynFlowNet checkpoint and samples synthesizable molecules on GPU, returning one sample set per request with each molecule's canonical SMILES, synthesis route, consumed building blocks, and per-molecule metrics (reward, number of reactions, QED, and molecular weight).
+
+## Background
+
+Generative flow networks ([Bengio et al., 2021](https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.04399)) learn a stochastic policy that constructs objects through a sequence of actions and samples them in proportion to a reward, which yields diverse, high-reward candidates rather than collapsing onto a single mode. SynFlowNet ([Cretu et al., 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.01155)) adapts this framework to molecular design by replacing the usual atom- or fragment-level action space with a *synthesis* action space: the policy selects building blocks and applies reaction templates step by step, so the terminal state is a molecule together with the reaction pathway that produces it.
+
+Because molecules are built only through valid reactions over purchasable reactants, SynFlowNet-generated candidates are synthesizable by construction, avoiding the common failure mode of generative models that propose structures no chemist can readily make. The sampler is guided by a task reward during training; the upstream repository ships reward functions including an sEH binding proxy, QED, Vina docking, and oracles from PyTDC, and new objectives can be added. Sampling in proportion to reward trades off potency against diversity, which is useful when the goal is a varied shortlist of viable candidates rather than a single optimum.
+
+### Learning Resources
+
+- [SynFlowNet repository](https://github.com/mirunacrt/synflownet) - source code, reward tasks, training scripts, and the building-block and reaction-template data used for sampling.
+- [SynFlowNet paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.01155) (Cretu et al., ICLR 2025) - the method, synthesis action space, and benchmark results.
+- [recursionpharma/gflownet](https://github.com/recursionpharma/gflownet) - the GFlowNet training codebase SynFlowNet builds on.
+
+## Tools
+
+### SynFlowNet Molecule Sampling (`synflownet-sample`)
+
+Samples synthesizable small molecules from a trained SynFlowNet GFlowNet, producing one sample set per request. Each request may carry an optional named reward `objective` (for checkpoints trained to switch objectives) and a free-form `label` echoed back on the matching sample set; a bare string or list of strings is coerced into requests carrying those objectives, and empty input yields a single default request.
+
+#### Applications
+
+Use this tool to generate a diverse, synthesizable candidate library for hit finding or lead expansion, to propose molecules optimized toward a trained objective such as an sEH binding proxy or QED, or to seed downstream docking, property prediction, and filtering workflows with structures that already come with a proposed synthesis route. Sampling multiple requests in one call is convenient for sweeping objectives or generating independent, reproducible batches.
+
+#### Usage Tips
+
+- **`model_checkpoint` is user-provisioned.** No trained weights are publicly distributed, so the checkpoint must be trained with the upstream repository. `"default"` loads the checkpoint placed in the managed weights cache; any other value is treated as an explicit path to a `.pt` checkpoint. A local checkpoint path is not available on hosted workers, so run locally with `device="cuda"` when pointing at your own file.
+- **Raise `num_molecules_per_request` for larger libraries.** Each request returns up to this many molecules; with `unique_molecules=True` the sampler oversamples and deduplicates canonical SMILES, and a low-diversity checkpoint may return fewer than requested after the dispatch cap, which is logged as a warning.
+- **Tune `sampling_temperature` to trade potency against diversity.** This is the GFlowNet inverse-temperature (beta): higher values concentrate sampling on high-reward molecules, while lower values broaden chemical diversity. Combine with `random_action_prob` for additional exploration when samples collapse onto a narrow region of chemical space.
+- **Use `seed` for reproducible batches.** When omitted, the framework draws a seed and advances it across every dispatch so that duplicate requests still diverge; reuse the returned seed with the same checkpoint and configuration to reproduce a run.
+
+## Toolkit Notes
+
+<a href="https://bio-pro.mintlify.app/tools/guides/tool-persistence"><img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/Tool_Persistence_→-046e7a?style=flat-square&logo=readthedocs&logoColor=white" alt="Tool Persistence guide"></a> <a href="https://bio-pro.mintlify.app/tools/guides/device-management"><img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/Device_Management_→-046e7a?style=flat-square&logo=readthedocs&logoColor=white" alt="Device Management guide"></a> <a href="https://bio-pro.mintlify.app/tools/guides/parallel-execution"><img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/Parallel_Execution_→-046e7a?style=flat-square&logo=readthedocs&logoColor=white" alt="Parallel Execution guide"></a> <a href="https://bio-pro.mintlify.app/tools/guides/cloud-inference"><img src="https://img.shields.io/badge/Cloud_Inference_→-046e7a?style=flat-square&logo=readthedocs&logoColor=white" alt="Cloud Inference guide"></a>
+
+These apply to the SynFlowNet tool in this toolkit (`synflownet-sample`).
+
+- **Runs on GPU.** Sampling the GFlowNet policy uses a CUDA device; raise `batch_size` for throughput and lower it if the run exhausts GPU memory.
+- **A trained checkpoint is required; no public weights exist.** SynFlowNet does not ship trained model weights. Train a checkpoint with the upstream repository and provision it (as the managed `"default"` checkpoint or an explicit `.pt` path) before sampling.
+- **Building blocks and reaction templates ship with the upstream package.** The purchasable building-block set and reaction templates that define the synthesis action space come from SynFlowNet itself, so no separate chemistry database download is required for sampling.
+- **Molecules are synthesizable by construction.** Every generated molecule is assembled through valid reactions over the building-block set, so its reported synthesis route and consumed building blocks describe a concrete way to make it.
